Refinement types in Sounio allow you to constrain values at the type level. A refinement type is a base type plus a predicate that the value must satisfy.

Syntax: `type Name = { var: BaseType | predicate }`

Examples:
```sio
// A probability must be between 0 and 1
type Probability = { p: f64 | p >= 0.0 }

// A non-zero integer for safe division
type NonZero = { n: i64 | n != 0 }

// Used in function signatures:
fn safe_div(a: i64, denom: { d: i64 | d != 0 }) -> i64 with Div, Panic {
    a / denom
}
```

Refinement types are checked at compile time when possible (constant values) and at runtime otherwise (a Panic effect may be needed). They prevent entire classes of bugs — you cannot accidentally pass zero to a function expecting NonZero.

This is a distinguishing feature of Sounio: most languages handle this with runtime checks or not at all. Sounio makes the constraint part of the type itself.
